# Minimal change

- Before changing code, list everything the change must reach: callers, tests, fixtures, config and exports. Finish all of it.
- Do not add features, options, abstractions, wrappers or "for later" code that nobody asked for.
- Reuse the helper, component or pattern the codebase already has before writing new code, and use it the way the surrounding code does.
- Prefer the standard library or a dependency the project already has. Do not add a dependency for a few lines.
- Comment only the why that the code cannot show, in one line.
- When fixing a bug, find every caller of the function you touch, then fix the root cause once in the shared code.
- A shortcut with a known limit gets a comment in this form: `# shortcut: <the limit>, <when to upgrade>`.

Why: every line added is a line someone must read, test and maintain, so the smallest change that fully solves the task is the cheapest one over time. Small does not mean unfinished: skipping a caller or a test the change breaks only moves the cost to someone else, and a shortcut with a stated limit tells the next reader when it stops being enough.

## Checks
- enforced-by: shortcut-comment-format
- enforcement: llm-review
